`VersionedProcessGroup` is missing two fields from the cited `nifi-api` model:

```java
private String statelessFlowFileContentInMemoryMax;
private Integer statelessFlowFileContentInMemoryHeapPercentage;
```

Since this file is intended to model the complete graph reachable from `RegisteredFlowSnapshot`, please add both as optional fields:

```ts
statelessFlowFileContentInMemoryMax?: string;
statelessFlowFileContentInMemoryHeapPercentage?: number;
```

Both are nullable in Java, so optionality is appropriate. Without them, a valid snapshot is silently narrowed and cannot be round-tripped at full fidelity.

These identity fields are optional even though `DtoFactory.createProcessorStatusDto` populates them on every response:

```java
dto.setName(procStatus.getName());
snapshot.setName(procStatus.getName());
snapshot.setType(procStatus.getType());
```

Under this PR's response-only contract, I believe these should be required:

```ts
export interface ProcessorStatusDTO {
    name: string;
    // `type` remains optional: the factory does not set it on the outer DTO.
}

export interface ProcessorStatusSnapshotDTO {
    name: string;
    type: string;
}
```

The same factory pattern appears for connection, port, process-group, and remote-process-group status names. Could those status DTOs be tightened consistently as part of this Java-vetted model?

`allowableValue` is currently optional:

```ts
export interface AllowableValueEntity {
    allowableValue?: AllowableValueDTO;
    canRead: boolean;
}
```

Unlike `BulletinEntity`, `EntityFactory.createAllowableValueEntity` always sets its nested body, including when `canRead` is false:

```java
entity.setCanRead(canRead);
entity.setAllowableValue(dto);
```

The descriptor factory also constructs the DTO before creating every entity. Please make this field required:

```ts
allowableValue: AllowableValueDTO;
```

This distinction matters because permission gates are field-specific; carrying bulletin-style optionality here weakens a body that NiFi always supplies.
